{
  "requirement": "`kind` classifies the construct and `span` locates it under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.span`. Language providers can\nconstruct an attachment from these two fields.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct OutlineAttachment {\n    pub kind: AttachmentKind,\n    pub span: Span,\n}\n\nimpl OutlineAttachment {\n    pub fn new(kind: AttachmentKind, span: Span) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for OutlineAttachment;\nimpl Debug for OutlineAttachment;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.sdk.outlines.OutlineAttachment`. The span semantics belong to the cited `attachments.span` and are not reassessed here. I read the two marked tests and searched `crates` for other uses of `OutlineAttachment`.\n\nObligations from the subject text:\n1. The struct is `#[non_exhaustive]` with public fields `kind: AttachmentKind` and `span: Span`.\n2. `OutlineAttachment::new(kind, span) -> Self` lets language providers construct an attachment from those two fields.\n3. `OutlineAttachment` implements `Clone` and `Debug`.\n\nCoverage:\n- **Construction and field values:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[custom_attachment_spans_are_checked_against_the_original_snapshot]` builds attachments through `OutlineAttachment::new(AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\".into()), *span)` in a custom `OutlineLang`. After loading, it reads the public fields from `snapshot.outline().items[1].attachments[0]`. It asserts `attachment.kind == AttachmentKind::Other(\"marker\")` and `&snapshot.text()[attachment.span] == \"é\"`. This shows `new` stores both arguments in the right fields and that `kind` and `span` are public and readable. Using a custom `Span` of 1..3 and a non-default kind rules out swapped or defaulted fields. The test also checks `span` as the cited `attachments.span` text requires: non-char-boundary, out-of-range and past-the-item spans are rejected, and spans in the wrong order are rejected.\n- **Declaration shape, `non_exhaustive`, and trait impls:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` generates rustdoc for the crate and runs `check_declaration` against each requirement's Rust listing. It compares the struct declaration, field types and the `new` signature by `signature_parts`. It asserts `#[non_exhaustive]` for any struct with public fields. It compares the listed trait implementations (`impl Clone for OutlineAttachment`, `impl Debug for OutlineAttachment`) with rustdoc's trait-implementation section through `trait_impls`. Removing or adding `Clone` or `Debug`, changing a field type, or changing `new` would fail this test.\n\nRemaining points:\n- `Clone` and `Debug` are derived. Their presence is checked by the rustdoc comparison, and the requirement states no behavior beyond their existence.\n- `new` just passes its two arguments through, and the custom-language test also asserts the outcome.\n\nI found no obligation of this requirement that lacks a test, and no relevant acknowledgment or `{fixme}` is needed.",
